questions.question:
How do you turn an element into a flex container?
```css
.row {
  /* ??? */
}
```

questions.answer:
Set `display: flex` (or `inline-flex`). Its **direct children** become flex items.
```css
.row {
  display: flex;
}
```

It makes the **main axis vertical**, so items stack top to bottom. `justify-content` then works vertically and `align-items` horizontally.
```css
.stack {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
}
```

questions.question:
Which property spaces items along the **main axis**?
```css
.row {
  display: flex;
  /* ??? */: space-between;
}
```

questions.answer:
`justify-content`
```css
.row {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: space-between;
}
```
Other values: `flex-start`, `center`, `flex-end`, `space-around`, `space-evenly`.

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
}
```
Or `place-content: center` with `display: flex` on the parent.

Lets items **wrap onto new lines** instead of shrinking to fit on one line (the default is `nowrap`).
```css
.tags {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 8px;
}
```

`gap` sets the space **between** items, not around the edges.
```css
.row {
  display: flex;
  gap: 16px;
}
```
Before it, you needed margins on the items, which also add space at the ends.

questions.question:
What does this declaration mean?
```css
.item {
  flex: 1;
}
```

questions.answer:
The item's **starting size along the main axis**, before growing or shrinking. It overrides `width` (in a row) or `height` (in a column).
```css
.item {
  flex: 0 0 200px; /* grow shrink basis */
}
```

Use `align-self` on that item. It overrides the container's `align-items` for just that child.
```css
.row {
  display: flex;
  align-items: flex-start;
}
.special {
  align-self: flex-end;
}
```

Give it `margin-left: auto`: auto margins absorb the free space.
```css
.nav {
  display: flex;
}
.nav .login {
  margin-left: auto;
}
```
